WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.817 --> 00:00:05.900
IL-SUNG LEE: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.960
My name is Il-Sung Lee,
and I'm a product manager

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.090
within the Google Cloud
working on a key management

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.350
and encryption.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:14.260
Along with me, I have my
colleague, Joseph Valente,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.900
who is a product manager for
the Key Access Justification.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.090
And we're here to talk to
you about the new world

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.308
of controlling your data in
the cloud with EKM and KAJ.

00:00:23.308 --> 00:00:25.600
So in this session today
we're going to talk about some

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.120
of innovations in the
Google Cloud Platform

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.710
that's going to
help you really have

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.750
tight control over
your data on the Cloud

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.470
to an unprecedented degree.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.970
And by the end of
this presentation,

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.730
you'll understand
how the system works

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.330
and how it's going
to benefit you.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.452
And hopefully some of you will
actually give it a try as well,

00:00:41.452 --> 00:00:42.910
and understand how
you can actually

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.080
use it to protect your
most sensitive data.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.202
So in this talk, we'll have
three primary sections.

00:00:49.202 --> 00:00:51.410
First, we'll talk about the
current encryption that's

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.730
in GCP.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.480
And then followed by a
more in-depth discussion

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.712
around Cloud
External Key Manager.

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:00.170
And then after
that, I'll turn over

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.130
to Joseph, who's going to talk
a little more about the Key

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:04.773
Access Justification
product with you.

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:07.190
To start off with, let's talk
about the current encryption

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:08.930
in GCP.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.900
And usually I like to start
off with this the scale that

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.330
finds a control of the keys
and the products that we

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:15.770
have available.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.890
On the left hand side, we have
the situation where Google

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.060
has complete control over the
keys, and the right hand side,

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.580
it's a situation
where the customer

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.450
has control of the keys.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.730
So to start off on
the left hand side,

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.850
we're talking about
default encryption.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.600
And this is basically
Google's capability

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.810
of encrypting all your data
at rest, whatever it is

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.310
and whichever service.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.523
So whenever you bring
data up into our Cloud

00:01:42.523 --> 00:01:43.940
it's always
encrypted, and there's

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.500
no way to turn that off.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.680
And Google was the
first cloud to do this.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.510
And then for people who need
a little bit more control,

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.410
we introduced Cloud KMS.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.120
And Cloud KMS provides
a global service

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.510
that provides management
for your software keys,

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.650
and Cloud KMS performs
two primary services.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.920
One, it'll allow you to actually
do encryption, decryption,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.320
and signing for
whatever blossom data

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.100
that you provide through
our easy to use rest API.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.380
And two, it allows you to
integrate with other services,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:21.140
such as BigQuery, GCE, GCS,
Cloud Sequel, et cetera,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.990
that allows you to
take the data that's

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:25.970
encrypting those
services and ultimately

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:29.720
control it through a key that
you control in Cloud KMS.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.850
Now besides that,
we have Cloud HSM,

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.890
which is basically the
hardware version of Cloud KMS.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.300
And in cloud HSM,
you can actually

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.240
create keys and perform
all crypto-operations

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.480
within the fleet of HSMs
that Google maintains.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.090
And these HSMs are all fixed
140-2 level three devices.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.310
One of the nice properties
about putting keys in HSM

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.320
is that the hardware
will actually

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.050
make sure that the key
material is never exposed,

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.358
including to Google itself.

00:02:59.358 --> 00:03:01.400
Next to that we have
customer-supplied encryption

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.680
keys, which is one of
our first technologies

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.660
for key management.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.430
It was tailor made
for GCS and GCE.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.960
Essentially, in this
situation the data-at-rest

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.090
is protected by a key
that the application

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.740
has to provide
every time it tries

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.120
to access the data itself.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.132
Now it's fairly
limited, and you also

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:21.590
have to come to
terms with the fact

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.630
that the application
has to perform

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.060
some of the key management, and
also has access to the keys.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.520
And it probably won't be
expanded beyond GCS and GCE

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.226
for those reasons.

00:03:33.226 --> 00:03:35.300
And finally, we
have a new entry,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.520
which is a private host HSM.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.120
And this is an offering by
which if you have your own HSM

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.980
device that you
would like Google

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.000
to host in a colo as nearby
one of our data centers,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.550
because you have workloads
for example, life and shift

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.430
workloads, then this is
available for select customers.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.910
And it's something that
you can talk to me,

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.100
or talk to one of
our sales reps,

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.422
and get more information about.

00:04:00.422 --> 00:04:01.880
But even with those
offerings we're

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.010
still left with
the problem, how do

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.500
I retain possession and
control over my keys

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.960
while I'm still trying to
take advantage of the Cloud

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.110
for processing and
storage of my data itself?

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.830
So you have the Cloud with
all its immense computing

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.380
capabilities and
scale, but you still

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.300
want to have that tight
control over the data

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.980
itself through
control over the keys.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.330
What do you do?

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.730
So wouldn't it be
nice if you actually

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.460
had something beyond
the five offerings

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:29.530
that we have listed here?

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.750
Or something that perhaps goes
to the far right of this scale.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.180
And that's where a Cloud
External Key Manager fits in.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.170
The service is now
available as GA

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.320
in all regions around the
world, including multi-regions.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.010
And the service
is the first time

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:50.270
that a cloud is able to allow
you to separate your data fully

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.330
from your keys, and allow you
to essentially externalize

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.280
your keys outside the Cloud.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.530
Some people, including
analysts, are calling this hold

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.500
your own key, compared
to bring your own keys.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.760
But essentially, it's that.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.410
And so if we start talking
a little more about Cloud

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:09.740
External Key Manager,
you'll come to see quickly

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:11.510
what I'm talking about.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.905
But before we go into that,
let's backtrack a little bit.

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:16.280
And I want to talk a little
bit about Customer Management

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.700
Encryption Keys, and how the
keys hierarchy is essentially

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:21.150
set up.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.150
So in this jargon here
you'll see the fact

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.550
that when you bring data
up into the Cloud, let's

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.670
say it's a GCS object.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.760
What we'll do first of all, so
take that object most pleasant

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.910
to separate chunks.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:33.410
OK.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.827
We'll take into
manageable chunks,

00:05:34.827 --> 00:05:38.030
and each of those chunks will
have its own individual key

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.700
generated for its AS key.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.535
And once the key
is generated, it

00:05:43.535 --> 00:05:48.200
will encrypt that piece of
data, and these chunks of data

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.300
will be replicated.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.730
And then basically distributed
across all the servers

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.540
within our data centers.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.450
Now you can obviously
put that key

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.303
right next to the data itself.

00:05:57.303 --> 00:05:59.470
You need to actually make
sure that key's protected.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.280
So you encrypt it or wrap
it using some other key.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.660
So you encrypted with a key
encryption key, so to speak.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.120
And typically what
customers will do

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.590
is that they will
use a key that's

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:15.550
in our Cloud KMS, Cloud HSN, to
actually be the key encryption

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.860
key for that data
they have in GCS,

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.950
or a number of different
services as well.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.480
Now in the Cloud
External Key Manager

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.070
we have a situation where
you follow the same pattern,

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:31.540
except instead of protecting
with a key encryption

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:34.630
key that's in our KMS or
HSN, that key actually

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.250
resides outside.

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.870
And so if you see
the diagram here,

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.420
a user can actually
go in and either

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:44.290
put their data through in
BigQuery and Compute Engine.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.123
Or if they want to actually
direct encrypt the data

00:06:47.123 --> 00:06:48.790
through a symmetric
key that's external,

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.410
they can do that as well.

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.930
And these services communicate
directly with our KMS.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:58.030
Our KMS recognizes the fact that
these keys are not internal.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.120
That they're external.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.680
And then parlays request
alter our Cloud EKM,

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.040
which then communicates with
the External Key Manager.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.150
And so in the example I
gave earlier with GCS,

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:13.120
the data encryption key
is sent to KMS or HSN

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.710
to actually do encryption,
decryption, wrap, and unwrap.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:18.760
In this example, the
data encryption keys

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:20.992
will be sent to the
External Key Manager, which

00:07:20.992 --> 00:07:22.200
would do the wrap and unwrap.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.730
And send it back to us
through a secure channel.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.030
Now the key point here
is that that external key

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.390
that's doing the wrap and
unwrap is always outside.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:33.200
It's strictly outside of Google.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.400
And Google does not have a copy
or access to that key itself.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.370
So as you can see,
there's a clear boundary

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:42.040
now between the
data and the keys

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.280
themselves, which provides
you with that ideal security

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.580
separation.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.850
Now in terms of the
integrators that

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:54.510
have support for Cloud EKM,
there's five currently.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.420
Half of them are cloud
services, and other half

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.790
are providing
infrastructure services.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:04.200
So in terms of cloud services,
Equinix, Ionic, and Thales.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.800
They all provide cloud services.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.260
So you can sign up using their
web portal, create a key,

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.600
and it's basically a key
that they'll manage for you

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:13.560
in the Cloud.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.170
And then another half are
providing things like HSNs.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:20.280
So for Fortanix and
Thales both provide HSMs

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.460
that you can host in your own
data center, or on premise.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.820
And Unbound sells
software that you

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.980
install into your
own servers, and you

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.250
perform multipart computation.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.400
But then the question becomes,
why would I go through this?

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.860
And what are the real benefits
of External Key Manager,

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.360
if they're not obvious already?

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.350
So number one,
maintain key provenance

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:45.070
comes to mind right away.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:48.130
And what I mean by that is that
you'll have a situation now

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.150
where you, as the person who
owns the actual External Key

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:56.410
Manager, will have
really, really

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.780
strong and tight
knowledge around where

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.310
the key was generated,
where it's kept,

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.490
who has access, who made a copy.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:05.470
And you'll have your own
set of auto-logs that

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:07.187
keep track of all the usages.

00:09:07.187 --> 00:09:09.520
And these are all things that
would make your compliance

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.070
regulator very happy, and
make it very easy articulate

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.440
to them where the
key is in the fact

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.350
that you have ownership of it.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.750
To access control is
another really big motivator

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:21.940
for people to use Cloud EKM.

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:25.270
So as I've been explaining
in the previous slides,

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.370
the key is to actually
decrypted the data

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:29.650
arrest are always outside.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.090
OK?

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:34.930
So from that perspective, if
that key was ever withheld,

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:37.360
then there's no path by
which Google can actually

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.440
go and decrypt the data.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.760
So once data is at arrest,
it would stay arrest.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.110
Effective becomes crypto
shredded at that point,

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.820
until the external key
is made available again.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.490
And then the third reason is
centralized key management.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:55.300
And so in many cases,
there are situations

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.700
where a customer
may want to have

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.500
one centralized key
manager where they

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:02.140
implement one set
of policies for all

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.510
the keys in their company.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:06.670
And this may include
keys are in the Cloud,

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.990
in other clouds on Premise.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.540
And this gives you
a really nice way

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:14.020
of actually managing and keeping
the keys all in one place.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:18.290
And so hybrid cloud, multi
cloud, single policy.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:23.960
these are all reasons why you
may want to use Cloud EKM.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:27.570
Now in terms of configuring
the service, it's very easy.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.560
So in the diagram that you
see on the right hand side,

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:33.770
this is just basically basically
our UI for creating a key.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.230
So typically you've
seen the fact

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:37.670
that you can generate a key.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:38.760
You can import a key.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.110
But now you'll see that there's
an external manage key radio

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:42.960
button.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:46.010
If you select that,
then what happens

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:48.710
is that once you create
the key externally,

00:10:48.710 --> 00:10:50.750
you'll be given a URI.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.880
That will basically
define that key,

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.930
and then you come to our
dashboard, or our UI,

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.550
and select externally
managed key.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.100
And then you enter at the
very bottom of that page,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.560
you can see the key URI field.

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:04.490
You put that in there.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:07.370
You create, and for that
point forward, we'll

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.332
validate that the key
exists externally.

00:11:11.332 --> 00:11:13.040
And then from that
point forward, the key

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.550
will show up as if it's
any other key within KMS,

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.610
Now it'll look identical,
except for the fact

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.970
that if you list the keys
out under protection level,

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.852
it will say external rather
than software or hardware.

00:11:27.852 --> 00:11:29.810
A couple other things
you point out about this.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.580
This is only available
currently for symmetric keys.

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:37.910
And again, it's something that
is available in all regions.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:39.920
So that's a nice thing.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.600
Now there's a few
considerations I

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.260
like you to take away when
you actually use this as well.

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.270
First of all, these
things are outside

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:48.560
of Google infrastructure.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:50.390
So as a result, there's
additional points

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.090
of failure there introduced
as a result of this.

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:56.060
And if it's a situation
where this is really

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.440
a big concern for you.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.180
Where you need to
have something that

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.830
has a high degree
of availability,

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.140
a high degree of performance,
low latency, all that stuff.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.680
And you want to make sure
that's something that has

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:08.940
called Cloud Skill for example.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.060
Then it may be
something that's worth

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:15.350
considering to use Cloud
KMS, or Cloud HSM instead.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.310
In general, I actually
usually recommend

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.780
to my customers to think of
things very holistically.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.640
That when you have
data then you should

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:25.610
be able to categorize data
in terms of at least the most

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:29.390
critical, the high importance,
and maybe less importance.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:30.890
And based on that,
that whould drive

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.740
the type of key
management selection

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.110
that you make with
EKM perhaps being used

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.750
for your most critical data.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:41.178
Now errors that come from
the External Key Manager,

00:12:41.178 --> 00:12:42.720
for example, there's
a network error,

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:46.372
or the External Key Manager is
turned off for whatever reason.

00:12:46.372 --> 00:12:48.830
Then these are all going to be
manifested when you actually

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:50.900
try to use the key itself.

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:52.340
You'll get very
clear errors that

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.680
say that the key manager
is not accessible,

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.590
the keys been disabled,
things like that.

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.820
And the errors themselves
will show up as user errors.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:03.230
And they will show
up through directly

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:04.370
through the error message.

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.707
And they will also
be logged as well.

00:13:06.707 --> 00:13:08.290
So if you turn on
data access logging,

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.040
you'll see them in your logs.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.620
Now there's only one
URI per key as you

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.370
saw in the previous slide.

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:17.390
And the implication
of that is that you

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:21.500
need to be in control of the
availability of the External

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:23.280
Key Manager yourself.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:25.430
And so in that
case, you may have

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.790
to do something
like leverage VIPs,

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:32.390
or leverage things like
load balancers on your side,

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.420
to make sure that you have
a properly available system

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:38.510
that's making your keys
available for your production

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:39.840
workloads.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.370
And please, work very closely
with your extreme key manager

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.500
provider as they should be able
to provide you with guidance

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.287
around the best approach in
terms of choosing locations,

00:13:49.287 --> 00:13:50.870
how to set up your
system to make sure

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:53.880
that's highly available.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:58.130
Now I'd like to show you a quick
demo of how we use our Cloud

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:01.940
EKM together with Ionic to
protect data and BigQuery.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:04.010
And so in this demo
you'll see real quickly

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:07.160
how powerful Cloud EKM
can be in protecting

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:08.872
your data in the Cloud.

00:14:08.872 --> 00:14:10.580
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.290
is create a new key.

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:15.030
And so as I explained before,
you have three options.

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:17.250
You can use a
generated key, meaning

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:21.350
that the Q be created by
Google, either in our Cloud HSM

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:23.480
or Cloud KMS service.

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.610
Or you can actually
use key import

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:28.220
so that you can provide
your own key material.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:30.590
Or the third one is
externally managed key, which

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:32.530
is what we're interested in.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:34.310
And to select a
sterling match key

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.310
and you can give it
any name that you like.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:39.850
And as you can see, the
purpose and the key type

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:41.840
are greyed out,
because the only type

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:44.330
of key that Cloud EKM
supports at the moment

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:47.640
are our symmetric keys.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:51.380
So at the very bottom, there's
a field called the key URI.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:53.990
And this is the only other
thing that you have to provide.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:56.370
When you create the key
index External Key Manager,

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:58.610
they'll provide you
with this URI, which

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:00.620
uniquely identifies a key.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:04.040
And in this case, I've already
created a key in Ionic machine.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.010
And so I'm just going to paste
in here, and then hit create.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.740
So just like that the
key's been created.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.830
And what's happened
underneath the covers

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:19.350
is that not only have
we cataloged that key

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.870
within our Cloud KMS,
but we've actually

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:24.720
gone through and validated and
tested that key to make sure

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:26.290
that it exists.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.980
So now I'm going move
over to BigQuery.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:34.080
So you can easily go
and create your own data

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:38.040
set or your table.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:41.490
And you can associate
it with that key

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:43.630
by simply going to
advanced options,

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.932
saying custom manage key,
and then choosing your key.

00:15:46.932 --> 00:15:48.390
If it doesn't show
up, you can just

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:52.290
simply enter the key ID directly
and just put it in here.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.970
But I've already done
this to save some time.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.060
And as you can see
here, when I actually

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.050
run the results comes back.

00:15:59.050 --> 00:16:00.750
It's nothing really interesting.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.310
But this data in order to be
able to be decrypted and then

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.830
the query run, actually
went externally

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:12.167
to Ionic to get the data
encryption key decrypted.

00:16:12.167 --> 00:16:14.250
And they came back so that
we can decrypt the data

00:16:14.250 --> 00:16:15.990
and show you the results set.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:18.360
Now to prove that this is
actually what happened,

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:20.910
I'm going to go to
Ionic's dashboard.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:25.230
And I'm going to disable this
key just I have associate

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:27.400
with the BigQuery data set.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:30.660
So now that that's been disabled
let me go back to BigQuery

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:34.240
and try running that
exact same query again.

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.430
And so you can see that we
return to error right away,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:40.980
and the error message says
external key provider message.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.950
So the message came directly
from the EKM that state

00:16:44.950 --> 00:16:47.260
that the operation was denied.

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:49.890
And so this is
basically a message

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.830
that's coming directly from the
External Key Manager stating

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:53.980
that the key was denied.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:56.760
And therefore, because we
couldn't use external key

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:00.570
to actually unwrap the
data encryption key,

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.250
and therefore, we couldn't
actually run the query.

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:07.050
So from our perspective,
from Google's perspective,

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.690
the data is no
longer accessible.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:12.210
Now to re-enable that
all we simply have to do

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.150
is just re-enable
this key policy.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:17.800
And it's over right now.

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.670
And I go back, and I hit run
for the same query again.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:23.040
And now the data
is available again.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.680
So this is sort of a
quick demonstration

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:29.430
to show how tightly linked the
ability to actually decrypt

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:35.550
the data and run the query is
to the key that's external.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:39.120
So now that you see how
easy and powerful using

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:41.250
Cloud EKM is with BigQuery.

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:44.130
And it is the same
with GCE as well.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:45.840
I'd like to turn it
over Joseph, who's

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:48.180
going to talk about key
access justification,

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.237
and you'll see how
the two together

00:17:50.237 --> 00:17:52.320
will take it to the next
level in terms of control

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:53.687
of raw data on the Cloud.

00:17:53.687 --> 00:17:55.020
JOSEPH VALENTE: Thanks, Il-Sung.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:57.460
As Il-Sung mentioned,
I'm Joseph Valente,

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:59.460
the product manager for
key access justification

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:00.608
here at Google Cloud.

00:18:00.608 --> 00:18:03.150
And I'm really excited to talk
to you about this new product,

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:04.920
and why we think it's
a huge innovation.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.500
I'm going to give an overview
of how this product works,

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.300
and hopefully talk
through why it's

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:10.800
such a huge benefit
to you if you're

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:13.760
using Excel Key Manager on GCP.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:15.360
And as part of
this walk through,

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:16.860
I want you to
imagine that you're

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:19.500
a person who's
using a query today,

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.900
and you're just using the
standard day forward encryption

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.590
with Google.

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:25.150
And you go ahead
and run a query.

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:28.000
And the BigQuery software
needs to decrypt your data,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.960
because it's
encrypted by default.

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:31.683
And so it's going
to go ahead and call

00:18:31.683 --> 00:18:33.100
our internal key
management system

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:34.960
and say, please decrypt
this data for me

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.353
so I can run the query.

00:18:37.353 --> 00:18:38.770
The internal key
management system

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:40.980
will go ahead and
authorize that decryption.

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:45.010
The BigQuery software
will perform the query,

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:48.700
and then we're done.

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.410
If you want more control
though over this process,

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.500
you may want to instead
of using the default

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.320
encryption, as Il-Sung discussed
earlier in the presentation,

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.570
have more control by
using our Cloud KMS

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:02.380
service, or our Cloud
key management service.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.120
This key management
service allows you

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.620
to control the keys yourself.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.120
And if you delete
one of these keys,

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:09.720
then there's no longer any
ability for the service

00:19:09.720 --> 00:19:12.572
to access your data.

00:19:12.572 --> 00:19:14.280
Even when you're using
this, though there

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:17.430
remains two pathways
that are often of concern

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.560
to customers who are
using the absolute most

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:22.640
sensitive operations on GCP.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:25.970
And those are the
administrators who are still

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:27.780
needing to run the
BigQuery software,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.900
as well as the key
management service,

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.010
we obviously lock down
administrative access

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.570
very, very, very tightly.

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:36.670
There's only a very
small number of people

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.280
who are able to exercise
administrative privileges.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:41.600
The things that they can
do are very, very limited.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.370
You can also use tools
like our apps approval tool

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.230
to put yourself in the
approval loop for these kinds

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:49.120
of administrative operations.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:54.040
But the concern that is in the
most very, very sensitive cases

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:55.430
is often too much for people.

00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:58.687
And so they would prefer to
not have the concern at all.

00:19:58.687 --> 00:20:00.520
And that's where our
External Key Management

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:02.730
service comes in.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.720
Once you use the cloud
External Key Manager

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:08.010
service to host your keys,
our key manager administrators

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:10.640
no longer have access to
the key encryption key,

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.030
as Il-Sung was
discussing before,

00:20:12.030 --> 00:20:16.770
which is now living inside your
External Key Manager service.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.053
Obviously, that comes
with a set of trade offs.

00:20:19.053 --> 00:20:20.970
You need to continue
working with your partner

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:23.070
to manage the
availability of this key,

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.690
and to make sure
that you're meeting

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.070
your goals in that space.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.340
But the trade off in
some limited cases

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.190
is obviously worthwhile
for these operations.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:34.380
And so if you're using
the key managed service,

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:35.550
you get this benefit.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:37.440
But there's one
piece missing, which

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:40.500
is that requests coming into the
External Key Manager service.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.300
And you need to
still have visibility

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.760
over why those are happening.

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:46.830
They can happen
for three reasons.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:50.130
You are running a query,
the BigQuery software itself

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:53.110
is performing some operation,
like it's doing a compression,

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:57.510
or a data optimization so that
you can run your queries more

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:00.450
efficiently later, or there's
some administrative operation

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:01.020
happening.

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:03.270
So you've opened up for
example, a ticket with Google.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:05.670
And you said, I'm experiencing
some performance issues,

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.962
and I need you to go ahead
and take a look at something

00:21:07.962 --> 00:21:09.330
and tell me what's going on.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.820
And what key access
justifications gives you is it

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.190
gives you visibility over
each of these reasons

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:15.750
through a justification
that comes

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.510
through with every request that
is made to your External Key

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:19.583
Manager service.

00:21:19.583 --> 00:21:21.000
What we're now
going to say is how

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.610
you can use blocking
policy on these requests.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:27.200
When each request
comes through you

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.330
can set a policy at your
External Key Manager

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:34.700
that contains any combination of
so allow denials that you want.

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:37.550
You can say, for example,
block all access,

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:39.980
and the key will become
completely unavailable.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:42.990
And Google will have no
access to data whatsoever.

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.740
You can block just
the administrator

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:48.633
rows that are the four green
rows at the bottom here.

00:21:48.633 --> 00:21:50.300
And that will stop
Google administrators

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:53.870
from having access to your
data even though you'll still

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.690
be able to continue using
customer initiated or system

00:21:56.690 --> 00:22:00.110
operations to continue
to using the service.

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:02.060
And what this gives
you is the ability

00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:04.940
to become the ultimate arbiter
of access to your data.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:09.080
You are the person who is
the ultimate decider of when

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:11.030
your data can be accessed,
and for what reason

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:12.435
it can be accessed.

00:22:12.435 --> 00:22:14.060
So what we're excited
to announce today

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:16.190
is that key access
justifications is now

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:18.500
in beta for Google
Compute Engine, Persistent

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:21.710
Disk, and BigQuery covering the
transition from data at rest

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:23.610
to data in use.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.383
And the exciting part
about the software

00:22:25.383 --> 00:22:27.800
is that when you use it together
with External Key Manager

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:31.220
you can become the ultimate
arbiter of access to your data.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:33.997
And this works together with
the integrity commitments

00:22:33.997 --> 00:22:36.080
that are contained in our
privacy website as well.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:37.820
So you know that
Google doesn't have

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:40.640
any kind of backdoor access,
or other ways of getting access

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:41.630
to your data.

00:22:41.630 --> 00:22:43.670
It really works the
way it's described.

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:46.220
We need to come to you and
query your External Key

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.440
Manager to decrypt your
data, and we give you

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:49.968
a reason when the
decryption happens

00:22:49.968 --> 00:22:52.510
that you can use to determine
whether or not grant us access.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:58.110
What does this mean for you?

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:01.260
You become the ultimate
arbiter of access to your data.

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:03.090
No other cloud provider
is offering you

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:04.620
this level of control.

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:06.420
Your keys are going
to live outside Google

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.128
with no administrative
access, and you'll

00:23:08.128 --> 00:23:09.660
have visibility
over what those keys

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:11.848
you access with
the ability to deny

00:23:11.848 --> 00:23:13.140
Google's access for any reason.

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:20.230
So the fine print, can
you turn this on yourself?

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:21.000
Not right now.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.160
We have a blog post
that you can head to,

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:26.460
which has a link on it where
you can express your interest.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.530
And we'll be in touch
with you, and consider

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:32.330
whether you're eligible
based on variety of criteria.

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:34.700
You will need to use
Google's External Key Manager

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:37.540
product to use key
access justifications.

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.290
For the reasons we've
described, obviously

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:41.000
the great benefit
comes from having

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.570
the keys outside of Google.

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:46.940
And so yes, while there
are awesome trade that

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:49.790
are involved in choosing whether
you use External Key Manager,

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:52.658
we think that for those very,
very sensitive operations,

00:23:52.658 --> 00:23:54.200
those are the ones
where you're going

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:58.530
to want to consider using key
access justifications as well.

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:01.640
So what data is in scope for
key access justifications?

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:04.100
Generally speaking, it's
the high sensitivity data.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:06.620
So you can think about it as
things like the BigQuery table

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:09.380
data, but not your
BigQuery query stream.

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:11.480
And so you should
read the documentation

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.940
when you're on boarded
and consider these things

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:16.940
when you're making the
decision of what information

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:19.160
you want to octet as
part of this sets.

00:24:22.717 --> 00:24:24.800
I'm now going to walk
through a demonstration that

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:28.280
talks about how you can set
up key access justifications,

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:32.550
and use it with some
of our partners.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:35.250
Let's take a look at how I can
use a variety of External Key

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:38.700
Management tools to set block
and policy that uses key access

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.500
justifications.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:42.540
Here I am using the
Thales tool to set

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:45.110
the set of allowed key
access justifications.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:47.610
And now I'm going to try and
do the same thing with the tool

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:48.180
by Fortenix.

00:25:01.528 --> 00:25:03.320
I'm also going to try
and do the same thing

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:05.360
using the Ionic tool,
which I'll continue

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:09.030
using for the rest of the
purposes of this demonstration.

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.960
As you can see, any partner that
supports the use of key access

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:15.260
justifications will allow you
to use these justifications

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:17.570
to gain access to
your keys based

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:20.810
on the inbound requests that
contain those justifications.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:22.370
Let's go ahead and
use the example

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:24.680
of running a query
using BigQuery

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.910
with a table that's encrypted
by an externally managed key.

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:30.620
I press the Run button, and
the query is successful.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:33.530
And when I go to my excel
key manager, I can see why.

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:35.180
The customer
initiated access value

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:37.750
was used to decrypt my table.

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:39.760
And because I've
allowed that value,

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:42.095
the query was successful.

00:25:42.095 --> 00:25:44.720
Later on in the day I'm going to
go ahead and check my External

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.900
Key Manager again.

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:48.910
Here I can see that
a new decryption

00:25:48.910 --> 00:25:50.680
request has been granted.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.223
This time for Google
initiated system operation,

00:25:53.223 --> 00:25:54.640
because BigQuery
needed to do this

00:25:54.640 --> 00:25:57.610
in order to compress my data and
perform further optimizations.

00:25:57.610 --> 00:25:59.260
Now if I were to
have an issue where

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.990
I needed to contact
Google support and Google

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:04.775
support needed to make
an access to my data,

00:26:04.775 --> 00:26:06.400
they would need to
use this tool, which

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.860
validates the justification,
or validates the case

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:10.750
number that I've opened,
to see whether they

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:13.240
should have access.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:15.970
When I go ahead and press
the send button here

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:18.590
you'll see that the
access gets denied.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.570
If I go back to my
External Key Manager page,

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:23.180
I can see that a request was
made with customer initiated

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:24.580
support as the reason.

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:26.480
But because I'm
denying that value,

00:26:26.480 --> 00:26:28.977
they were unable to
make that access.

00:26:28.977 --> 00:26:30.560
I actually do need
to give them access

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:32.820
though, because I really
need them to check this out.

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:34.278
So I'm going to go
ahead and delete

00:26:34.278 --> 00:26:37.130
that customer initiate support
value from the deny statement.

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:40.040
If the administrator tries
to make the access now again

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:42.050
using the same
support case number,

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:43.820
it would be validated
first on our side,

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:45.650
checking whether that
case is a real case,

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:46.880
and whether it's
open and assigned

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:49.380
to the person in question, and
whether the administrator has

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.030
the appropriate privileges.

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:53.850
But then it's also evaluated
again by the case manager,

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:55.278
and this time it's allowed.

00:26:55.278 --> 00:26:57.320
And I can see the customer
initiated support here

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:58.223
in the values column,

00:26:58.223 --> 00:27:00.140
So hopefully you've got
a better understanding

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:02.840
of how key access justifications
and External Key Manager

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:05.660
work together in practice to
make you the ultimate arbiter

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:06.710
of access to your data.

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:08.180
And why that's such
an important thing

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.472
to have when you're putting
very, very highly sensitive

00:27:10.472 --> 00:27:14.153
information on the Cloud.

00:27:14.153 --> 00:27:15.570
What are some of
the key takeaways

00:27:15.570 --> 00:27:18.540
we want you to leave
with this presentation?

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:22.947
First of all is keep your data
in our cloud, not your keys.

00:27:22.947 --> 00:27:25.280
You're going to get this
unprecedented level of control,

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:26.870
and keep possession
of your keys.

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:29.810
Use predictive data using
the External Key Manager

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:32.420
to store them outside of GCP.

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:34.550
We also have an upcoming
Cloud EKM white paper

00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:35.780
that you should look out for.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:37.905
It's going to talk about
some best practices on how

00:27:37.905 --> 00:27:40.630
to architect the Cloud EKM
with GCP so that you can

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:43.130
take into account some of the
reliability concerns you might

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:44.960
have.

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:48.020
Then, if possible, you should
use key access justifications

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:51.050
to get you that extra amount
of granularity and visibility

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:53.690
over access to your data through
the use of these key access

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:55.020
policies.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.200
And when you store your
keys outside of GCP,

00:27:57.200 --> 00:27:59.480
you can set these policies
up to deny Google access

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:00.357
for whatever reason.

00:28:00.357 --> 00:28:01.940
So you can continue
using your service

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:04.880
as you were beforehand, while
knowing that you're in control

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:07.280
and having the ability to
block Google for reasons

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.917
that you don't want.

00:28:08.917 --> 00:28:11.250
We hope you've really gotten
a lot out this presentation

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:11.750
today.

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:13.260
We're extremely
excited to continue

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:14.885
talking to you about
these new products

00:28:14.885 --> 00:28:17.770
as we continue to expand
our offerings in this space.

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:19.360
Thanks very much, and we look
forward to hearing from you

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:19.960
soon.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:21.810
Bye now.

